1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,000
The next drilling risk may already be written down—
in the report of a well drilled years ago.

2
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:16,000
eRTMAC shows what is happening now.
Historical lessons are buried in scattered reports.

3
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:24,000
NWIS connects those lessons to the interval ahead—
with the source beside the warning.

4
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:29,000
At 5 km, this synthetic demo returns one offset.
Radius is a first filter, not a geology match.

5
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:35,000
Expand to 50 km: three offsets.
Surface distance alone does not establish geological similarity.

6
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:40,000
Compare the reported formation intervals
on a shared measured-depth scale.

7
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:45,000
A historical pack-off is listed 32.9 m MD ahead
using approximate formation-top mapping.

8
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:50,000
Open marker 19 in the synthetic text report.
It is not a verified physical PDF page.

9
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:55,000
A generated training-only PDF becomes a structured event
with its physical page reference.

10
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:00,000
The extracted event starts in review.
An engineer must explicitly approve it.

11
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:05,000
After review, the event can enter historical context.
Its source remains attached.

12
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:10,000
The engineer records a decision for the active well,
citing an offset-well event.

13
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:15,000
The handover note survives reload.
Reviewer identity is self-entered, not authenticated.

14
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:45,000
The synthetic pack-off replay changes pressure, torque,
and penetration rate. This is not live eRTMAC telemetry.

15
00:01:45,000 --> 00:02:04,000
A labeled, uncalibrated heuristic surfaces an assessment.
No incident countdown or field performance is claimed.

16
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:21,000
Every well leaves a lesson.
The next well should not have to learn it again.
